---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/iron`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Iron Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: { '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Iron deficiency anaemia', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'D50', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Iron', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Iron: Deficiency Anaemia, Heme vs Non-Heme, Supplement Forms Compared"
  description="Iron reference — deficiency anaemia diagnosis, ferritin interpretation, heme vs non-heme absorption, bisglycinate vs sulphate, and haemochromatosis screening."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/iron", label: "Iron" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Iron</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Iron is a component of haemoglobin (which carries oxygen), myoglobin, cytochromes, and
      numerous enzymes. Iron deficiency anaemia affects roughly 25% of the world's
      population and is the most common nutrient deficiency globally.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily requirements</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adult men and post-menopausal women:</strong> 8 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Menstruating women:</strong> 18 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 27 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Vegetarians / vegans:</strong> 1.8× the standard RDA due to lower bioavailability of non-heme iron.</li>
      <li><strong>Tolerable Upper Intake Level:</strong> 45 mg/day (adults) from supplements — above this, GI toxicity is common.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Heme vs non-heme</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Heme iron</strong> — from animal muscle (beef, lamb, chicken, fish). Absorbed 15–35% regardless of other dietary factors.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-heme iron</strong> — from plants and fortified foods. Absorbed 2–20% depending on cofactors. Vitamin C in the same meal roughly doubles absorption; tannins in coffee/tea and calcium reduce it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deficiency and anaemia</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      Symptoms of iron deficiency (with or without anaemia): fatigue, exercise intolerance,
      pallor, brittle nails, restless legs syndrome, pica (ice craving), headaches, hair
      loss, cold intolerance. Anaemia (low haemoglobin) is a late sign — depleted iron
      stores (low ferritin) occur first.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Diagnosis:</strong> serum ferritin is the single best test. &lt; 30 ng/mL is
      generally considered deficient (some experts push the cut-off to &lt; 50 in
      symptomatic patients). Confirm with iron, TIBC, and transferrin saturation if uncertain.
      Investigate cause of iron loss in men and post-menopausal women — colorectal or upper
      GI blood loss must be ruled out.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Supplementation</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl shadow-sm mb-4">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-gray-50 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-200">
          <tr>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-3">Form</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-3">Elemental Fe</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 hidden md:table-cell">Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-gray-700">
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Ferrous sulphate 325 mg</td><td class="px-4 py-3">65 mg</td><td class="px-4 py-3 hidden md:table-cell">Standard first line; cheapest; GI upset common.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Ferrous gluconate</td><td class="px-4 py-3">36 mg/324 mg</td><td class="px-4 py-3 hidden md:table-cell">Gentler; may need higher pill count.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Ferrous bisglycinate</td><td class="px-4 py-3">28 mg per 200 mg pill</td><td class="px-4 py-3 hidden md:table-cell">Much better tolerated; higher absorption per mg elemental.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">IV iron (iron sucrose, ferric carboxymaltose)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,000 mg per dose</td><td class="px-4 py-3 hidden md:table-cell">For severe deficiency, intolerance, or malabsorption.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Contemporary evidence supports <strong>alternate-day dosing</strong> for repletion —
      65 mg every other day absorbs as much iron per week as 65 mg daily, with far less GI
      upset and less hepcidin blockade of subsequent absorption (Stoffel 2017).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Food sources</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Beef liver (3 oz): 5.2 mg heme</li>
      <li>Oysters (3 oz): 8 mg</li>
      <li>Beef, sirloin (3 oz): 2.5 mg heme</li>
      <li>Chicken thigh (3 oz): 1.1 mg heme</li>
      <li>Fortified breakfast cereal: 4–18 mg non-heme</li>
      <li>Lentils (½ cup): 3.3 mg non-heme</li>
      <li>Spinach (½ cup cooked): 3.2 mg non-heme</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Iron overload — the opposite risk</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Haemochromatosis</strong> (hereditary iron overload) affects ~1 in 200 people
      of Northern European descent. Screening with transferrin saturation and ferritin is
      appropriate in adults with unexplained fatigue, joint pain, liver enzyme elevation,
      or diabetes. Untreated haemochromatosis causes cirrhosis, cardiomyopathy, and diabetes.
      Never take a routine iron supplement without a documented indication.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Iron deficiency in adults warrants investigation
      of the cause. GI bleeding, coeliac disease, and menorrhagia are the most common
      culprits and must be ruled out.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/iron" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
